  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA weak wave evident in the water vapor loop was dropping south across\nMO/AR and this has caused sfc winds to back to the ESE across the\neastern half of the CWA where dewpoints have remains around 70. Weak\nsfc convergence appears to be generating a agitated cu field south of\nLyon County this afternoon and with an uncapped atmosphere after 3pm\nit appears that isolated convection could develop at least for a few\nhours before sunset south of Topeka. Dry conditions should prevail\nafter sunset with another very warm Sept night in store.  \n\nOn Tuesday, the low level thermal ridge will be drawn northeast \nacross the region in advance of the sfc front with another very hot \nday expected with highs around 100 and HI again in the 100-109 range.\nAs a result we will keep the heat warning in place through tomorrow.\nGiven instability along the boundary tomorrow afternoon there should\nbe scattered storms along the front which could impact north central\nKS during the afternoon and evening with a wind and marginal hail \nrisk. Additional elevated storms should form overnight into early \nWeds just north of the boundary so expect scattered storm potential \nto persist Tues night through Weds as the front sags southward across\nthe area. No plans to extend any heat headlines for Weds at this \npoint given uncertainty of frontal position and clouds/precip impacts\non temps. \n\nThe area may see several chances for storms in the Weds night through\nSaturday period with stronger 500mb flow and LLJ/WAA nighttime \nforcing especially Fri into Saturday.",
